LOESS reference implementation
------------------------------
packages/core/src/stats/loess.ts adapts its structure (the sorted
nearest-neighbor window walk and the tricube kernel) from SveltePlot's
`src/regression/loess.ts` (ISC License, Copyright (c) Gregor Aisch and
SveltePlot contributors), which is itself adapted from:

  - d3-regression, Copyright (c) Harry Stevens (MIT License)
    https://github.com/HarryStevens/d3-regression
  - science.js, Copyright (c) Jason Davies (BSD-3-Clause License)
    https://github.com/jasondavies/science.js
  - vega-statistics, Copyright (c) University of Washington Interactive
    Data Lab (BSD-3-Clause License)
    https://github.com/vega/vega

The ggsvelte implementation is substantially rewritten for parity with R's
stats::loess (span-based neighborhoods, configurable polynomial degree,
prediction at arbitrary points, and exact operator statistics for the
confidence band); no verbatim code from the above projects remains, but the
algorithmic lineage is acknowledged here per their license terms and the
project plan.

Kyoto cherry-blossom full-bloom dates (bundled teaching dataset)
----------------------------------------------------------------
packages/svelte/src/lib/data/kyoto-sakura.ts and the docs asset
apps/docs/static/kyoto-sakura.json contain peak-bloom dates for Kyoto,
Japan, 812–2026 CE.

  Data copyright holder: Yasuyuki Aono (Osaka Prefecture University).
  Source: http://atmenv.envi.osakafu-u.ac.jp/aono/kyophenotemp4/
  - 9th–14th centuries acquired and analyzed by Aono and Saito (2010),
    International Journal of Biometeorology 54, 211–219.
  - 15th–21st centuries acquired and analyzed by Aono and Kazui (2008),
    International Journal of Climatology 28, 905–914.
  - Modern observations are made by a local newspaper in Arashiyama, Kyoto.

The series was retrieved via the cleaned mirror published for the George
Mason University peak-bloom-prediction competition
(https://github.com/GMU-CherryBlossomCompetition/peak-bloom-prediction,
data/kyoto.csv), whose repository content is CC BY-NC-SA 4.0; the Kyoto
observations themselves carry Aono's copyright notice above. ggsvelte
redistributes the observation dates — factual measurements, reproduced
without the mirror's editorial content — with the attribution required by
the source. Anyone publishing charts made from this data should cite Aono
as above (see the KYOTO_SAKURA_CITATION export).

Binning, boxplot, density, and summary algorithms are independent ports of
the published ggplot2 (MIT) and R (GPL — algorithms only, no code copied)
semantics, verified against R-generated fixtures; see
docs/decisions/0010-m2-stats-notes.md.

Example gallery datasets
------------------------
Several datasets under examples/*/*/data.ts are transcribed from HistData
(https://github.com/friendly/HistData), an R package of historical datasets
in the history of statistics and data visualization, maintained by Michael
Friendly. The HistData package is distributed under the GPL; each dataset's
`data.ts` header names the dataset and its original source.

The underlying tables are digitizations of works published before 1930 —
among them Halley (1693), Playfair (1821, 1824), Quetelet (1846), Farr
(1852), Snow (1855), Nightingale (1858), Minard (1869), Guerry (1833),
Darwin (1876), Michelson (1879), Galton (1886) and Herschel (1833) — whose
originals are long out of copyright. ggsvelte makes no public-domain claim
about the HistData package itself; this notice credits HistData and its
maintainer as the proximate source of the transcriptions.

Individual datasets carrying additional provenance, credited where used:
  - Snow.* — modern digitization of Snow's Broad Street map by Rusty Dodson
    and Waldo Tobler
  - Guerry — digitization by Stephane Dray and Michael Friendly
  - Langren — reconstruction by Michael Friendly
  - Cholera — transcription by Nicholas Verlander
